# {{.BinaryName}}

{{.Description}}

A SoulModule bundle for Soul Stack, serving the `{{.Name}}` module. Implemented via `sdk/module` on top of the gRPC-stdio handshake ([ADR-020](https://github.com/souls-guild/soul-stack/blob/main/docs/architecture.md#adr-020-plugin-infrastructure-manifest-handshake-lifecycle-format)).

## The artifact has no name of its own

There is no `manifest.yaml`. The module declares itself as a `module.Def` value in `internal/{{.NamePackage}}/handler.go`, and the schema document is **generated** from it: `make stamp` puts one copy inside the binary and writes another to `dist/schema.json`.

The artifact carries no namespace and no publisher. Address level 1 is the **registration alias** an operator picks — register this artifact as `{{.Namespace}}` and a task writes `{{.Namespace}}.{{.Name}}.present`; register the same bytes as something else and the same module answers there instead.

## States

- `present` - the resource exists with the requested parameters.
- `absent` - the resource is absent.

Parameters - see `Module.States.<state>.Input` in `internal/{{.NamePackage}}/handler.go`.

Any `Secret: true` parameter must carry `Pattern: "^vault:.*"`; without it the bundle fails validation (`input_secret_without_vault_pattern`) at startup, in `make test`, and at `soul-mod verify`.

## Usage in destiny

```yaml
- name: create the resource
  module: {{.Namespace}}.{{.Name}}.present
  params:
    name: example
    # TODO: remaining params
```

`{{.Namespace}}` here is the alias the operator registered this artifact under, not something the artifact declares.

## Build

```
make build       # -> dist/{{.BinaryName}}
make stamp       # generate + stamp the schema document, write dist/schema.json
make verify      # stamped document == the document the code generates
make test
make check       # fmt + vet + test + stamp + verify
```

## Checking a destiny against this module, offline

```
soul-lint validate-scenario <path> --modules {{.Namespace}}=./dist/schema.json --service-name <service>
```

The alias goes on the flag for the same reason it is absent from the artifact: it is the operator's choice, so the linter has to be told which one the definition assumes.

## Sigil-trust

Before publishing an official plugin to production, an Archon signs the binary's
digest through Keeper ([ADR-026](https://github.com/souls-guild/soul-stack/blob/main/docs/architecture.md#adr-026-sigil---plugin-integrity-keeper-signed-digest-index)).

## Tests

- **L0** - unit tests with a mock `pluginv1.SoulModule_ApplyServer` (see `internal/{{.NamePackage}}/handler_test.go`), plus the bundle validation in `cmd/{{.BinaryName}}/bundle_test.go`.
- **L1** - integration via testcontainers against a real backend resource.
- **L3b** - real-soul-container (privileged Debian-12 + the module installed).

More details - [Plugin author guide](https://github.com/souls-guild/soul-stack-plugins/blob/main/docs/module-author-guide.md).

